you  welcome to Abbott's web series at Abbott  we know that a healthier Hospital begins  with a healthier laboratory because labs  performing at their best deliver results  that provide better patient outcomes and  improve healthcare value Abbott can help  you redefine performance with  personalized and innovative solutions we  hope you find Abbott's web series a key  resource in helping you achieve  measurably better healthcare performance  today's healthcare executives have many  issues weighing on their minds things  like reimbursement changes patient  satisfaction and population health  management just to name a few as a  result they have higher expectations for  all their departments including the  clinical laboratory in fact in a recent  global survey c-suite executives ranked  the laboratory as the second most  important department in delivering on  key performance indicators so what steps  should labs take to have a measurable  impact on operational excellence and  healthcare outcomes and how can labs  deliver maximum value to meet these  expectations hello I'm Jose Santiago  today we'll discuss how you and your lab  can continue to achieve operational  excellence and further contribute to  your healthcare institution with me  today is Paul Clarke who will discuss  the concept of the labs value hi Jose  glad to be here also with me is Patrick  gontar who will discuss the key factors  that drive cost and how they can  contribute to the underlying value of  the lab hi Jose happy to join you today  Paul let's start by talking about  realizing the labs value through  operational excellence two concepts  total value of ownership or TVO and  total cost of ownership or TCO these are  commonly used in other industries to  evaluate operational performance discuss  concepts are now making headway into the  laboratory setting  that's correct Jose in general value is  a difficult concept to quantify both  inside and outside the lab one way to  think about value is as an equation  which defines as the best possible  outcomes achieved at the lowest cost  today it's no longer enough for the lab  to simply deliver timely test results to  physicians just like the hospital health  system the lab must also demonstrate his  ability to contribute to positive  patient outcomes at the lowest possible  cost a UK study found that seventy to  eighty percent of all healthcare  decisions affecting diagnosis or  treatment are influenced by laboratory  results and while lab service is  typically made up between three and five  percent of a hospital's budget  the lab directly impacts 70% of  value-based performance areas and that's  that's a huge disconnect the critical  factors which affect how a hospital's  values measured are outcomes patient  experience efficiency safety and  clinical processes when the lab is  delivering to its full potential it can  help improve outcomes system-wide  allowing the institution to positively  impact their patients and the community  at large that's why it's extremely  important to avoid marginalizing the lab  we need to find ways to integrate the  lab more tightly into the overall  umbrella of healthcare decision making  and that essentially is how you change  the healthcare value equation in your  favor  it's also why I'll be talking about the  TVO methodology today and that's as a  way to ensure the lab is operating in  the most efficient way possible yes I  agree it's not always easy for  laboratorians to demonstrate their value  to the broader healthcare system but if  they truly could assess and communicate  their value they would be better  prepared to deliver on the high  expectations of the healthcare  management so let's discuss how labs can  go about assessing and communicating  their value using the TVO methodology  sure ensure TVO focuses on maximizing  operational efficiency taken into  account factors that go beyond the  direct and indirect costs of running a  system or instrument and the benefits  they produce as an example one factor is  space which is a precious commodity  regardless of where you are in the world  when a laboratory can optimize how it  uses its space it becomes possible to  bring additional testing in-house and  add needed instrumentation ultimately  something that may seem simple like  maximizing the use of space in your lab  to add a new test capability could help  you improve clinical care and elevate  the value of the lab to the healthcare  organization by conducting a TVO  assessment laboratories can find ways to  improve operational productivity and  efficiency and they can lead to  downstream clinical care benefits thanks  Paul we now better understand how TVO  helps uncover the true value of the lab  helping it to stay viable in the face of  rising costs budget cuts and pressures  to deliver more value to the overall  healthcare ecosystem so now let's turn  our attention to TCO or total cost of  ownership Patrick you've done a lot of  work in this area  can you help explain the concept of TCO  and how it differs from TV oh sure  total cost of ownership as it name  implies has to do with costs it is an  estimate and a calculation of all direct  costs for things like instruments and  reagents indirect costs such as employee  time energy water consumption  all of these contributes to the cost of  acquiring and operating an instrument  throughout its lifetime think about it  this way you can choose to invest in a  product that appears to be the most cost  effective option for an initial  investment perspective but if you  consider other elements such as how much  time it takes to maintain it you quickly  realize that the product may not be the  best option when you factor in all the  value of your extra time I completely  agree Patrick I'd add that we can  visualize TCO as a step towards TVO and  understanding a laboratory is overall  value TVO builds on TCO and complements  it with Lean Six Sigma processes to help  labs find a way to streamline their  operations and achieve the best outcomes  for their entire healthcare system in  other words TVO is more holistic because  it considers the additional benefits of  an investment or process and not just  costs alone furthermore TVO is all about  determining which decision will drive  the greatest value both from monetary  perspective as well as from a desire to  outcomes perspective so how can  assessing TVO help clinical laboratories  realize their value where we can look at  it in four key areas let's consider the  previous example where laboratory space  is one factor that can be modified to  improve the value of a lab first imagine  if a laboratory could do the same number  of tests in a smaller footprint or  rearrange the lab layout to bring in a  new test system that meets a particular  needs of the healthcare institution  think about the benefits this could have  a laboratory could be more nimble  bringing tests in-house that were  previously out sourced all by simply  reconsidering the labs physical space  secondly imagine if a lab found a way to  better use technician time rather than  performing mundane tasks such as  instrument maintenance technicians would  be able to provide more value added  benefits like improving the lab service  levels this could then bring additional  benefits to the broader healthcare  organization and you're doing all of  this just by looking at one factor  technician time thirdly improving  operational efficiency and effectiveness  not only helps save on costs it also  helps laboratories focus on the most  important aspects of care such as  increasing clinician and patient  satisfaction and then finally if a lab  gains more time to make data-driven  decisions it can ultimately help improve  patient outcomes and demonstrate is  value to the overall healthcare  organization I agree Pollan this is a  critical point going back to that global  study we mentioned previously 93 percent  of healthcare c-suite executives 