ich we  define as the time needed to process a  sample including the average time to  process that tubes and finally we  compared electricity consumption by the  platform with regards to maintenance we  found that alline TCI outperformed all  the other platforms in terms of downtime  for maintenance we also broke out the  maintenance time by the number of hours  the instrument was unavailable and by  the numbers of hours staff members were  engaged in managing the instrument or  human time as you can see here the  eliminated CI series required 37 to 56  percent less annual maintenance time  than the other four platforms Patrick  these are great results this could lead  to considerable time savings for a lab  and could in turn positively impact  other KPIs in a healthcare institution  exactly instrument maintenance is  something we know is necessary to ensure  our tests are generated properly but  when you extrapolate the amount of time  needed to maintain a competitive  instrument in relation to illinit e you  could save let's say forty forty three  thousand dollars a year as an example or  if you think of that in terms of value  that could be a full-time technicians  job all year long 2080 hours per year  just doing maintenance that's  significant Wow so that is an entire  year of an employees time just doing  maintenance I can think of many more  productive things that a staff could do  to help increase the value of the lab  Patrick let's move to your second  outcome processing time across the  platforms yes we found that the illinit  TCI series  had the fastest peak performance it had  a throughput of a hundred and seventy  tubes in hundred and seven minutes which  is up to 39 percent faster than the  other platforms we tested what's  interesting is the Rosco bus 8000 shown  here by the maroon line you can see that  while it had the fastest processing time  up to the first hundred and thirty  samples the speed then plateaued in a  system saturation phenomenon what  exactly do you mean by the system  saturation phenomenon and and how does  it translate in terms of impact to  laboratory operations system saturation  occurs when the time required to run the  tubes is much slower than the scanning  time at some point the analyzers reaches  an overflow situation it can't run the  entire number of scan tubes which cause  a delay in analysis and reporting so you  just described an interesting throughput  phenomenon in a typical lab we know  throughput can be affected by multiple  variables for example when stat samples  are introduced into a routine it could  affect the stat turnaround time did you  consider this scenario in your study as  well as a matter of fact yes we  simulated the disruption caused by the  stat samples by placing 12 additional  samples on the instrument in random  order during routine test panels being  run we saw an average of 41 percent  lower stat processing time across  multiple analytes on the LNT and  architects systems compared to the other  platforms we know that tad can affect  many KPIs that extend outside of the lab  such as physician and patient  satisfaction or even Hospital length of  stay so it's important to be confident  in the real-world efficiency of the  systems you're using the third driver of  operational efficiency evaluated in your  study was electricity consumption  correct yes again correct in comparing  the five platforms about salinity CICS  system consumed up to 79 percent less  electricity than the other four  platforms  I'm glad you tested this Patrik because  it's not only important for  organizations that are cost conscious  but nowadays people are more more  concerned with their effect on the  environment organizations pride  themselves on being green running lean  being eco-friendly and environmentally  sustainable  this is an important finding I agree  Patrick what are the key takeaways from  the study that you like to end with the  primary finding was that Abbott salinity 